WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.147
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.147 --> 00:00:04.980
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello,
I'm Dustin Kirkland,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.450
Product Manager at Google.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.660
And I'm joined here by one of my
colleagues, Jeremy Lewi, who's

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.300
going to tell us a
little bit about Kubeflow

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.180
and his talk at
KubeCon here this week.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.850
What can you tell us about
developing applications

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.380
on Kubeflow?

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.344
JEREMY LEWI: Hi.

00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:22.260
So what we learned at
Google is that if you're

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.770
building ML systems, you have
a complicated distributed

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.610
system that requires lot
of dev ops challenges.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.460
So with Kubeflow, we decided
to take advantage of containers

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.770
and Kubernetes to try to
build a ML platform that could

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.500
help solve these challenges.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.330
And at KubeCon,
what I'll be demoing

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.960
is an end-to-end example of
solving those challenges using

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.380
Kubeflow, building
a ML product where

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.740
the product, in this case,
is summarizing GitHub issues.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.820
So after deploying Kubeflow,
we can access JupyterHub

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.190
and use JupyterHub
to launch a notebook

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.590
for our initial
experimentation and analysis.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.834
Here I'm using a
custom notebook image

00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:02.250
that has all the
libraries we need

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.239
for this particular example.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.309
And I just click Spawn
to go ahead and spawn

00:01:06.309 --> 00:01:07.350
that particular notebook.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.570
Once Jupyter starts,
we can go ahead

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.030
and navigate through
the menus to find

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:16.710
our particular notebook.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.500
And this particular notebook
was developed by Hamal Hussein

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.160
for this particular example.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.460
And we can see that it
has all the steps needed

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.490
to develop a model.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.400
So first we download some data.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.560
Then we pre-process the data.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.540
Once we're done
pre-processing the data,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.840
we're going to go ahead and
define our model architecture.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.500
And we can display the
actual model architecture

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.210
using the notebook.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.080
Once we're done
developing the model,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.640
we can actually train the model.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.000
And you can see it's running
for a couple of iterations.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.458
Once we're done
training the model,

00:01:46.458 --> 00:01:49.560
we can generate some
predictions in the notebook.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.900
And this is great, but we only
used a subset of the data.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.410
So now we're going to go ahead
and train the model at scale

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.400
using distributed training.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.670
But first, to do that,
we have to take the code

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:03.250
and put it into a Python file.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.162
We can then use the Python
file to build the Docker image.

00:02:07.162 --> 00:02:08.620
So this is the
Python file with all

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.800
of the code, which is
basically just copied directly

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.887
from the notebook image.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.840
We can then go to
the command line,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.740
and we can deploy
a custom TensorFlow

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.950
job, which is a custom
resource in Kubernetes

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.160
that we created to make it easy
to run distributed training.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.500
So we've gone ahead
and deployed that job.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.170
And now we can look at the
actual spec for that job.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.200
And basically it's a bunch
of pod template specs, where

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.320
one pod template spec describes
each replica in our distributed

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.600
training job.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.060
While the job is
training, we can go ahead

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.430
and launch TensorBoard,
and use TensorBoard

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.770
to see how well our
model is training

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.450
and what the performance is.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.250
Once we're done
training the model,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.310
we can take advantage of
Seldon to deploy the model.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.900
And to use Seldon,
all we have to do

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.820
is implement the
predict function.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.710
We can then build the
Docker image with our model.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:06.960
We can then deploy it using
Seldon's custom resource.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.100
So here we're just launching
the Seldon deployment

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.654
for our particular model.

00:03:13.654 --> 00:03:15.320
After we've deployed
it, we can go ahead

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.310
and see what the spec is.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.170
And we can see that the spec
is very similar to a deployment

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:21.860
spec.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:25.850
The main difference is we can
specify an inference graph that

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:26.810
consists of the models.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.309
And once we're done
with the models,

00:03:28.309 --> 00:03:31.580
we can go ahead and check out
our actual web application

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.860
to generate predictions.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.850
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Summarizing GitHub issues.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.040
So you take as input--

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.900
your data input is, I
guess, a GitHub project.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.880
And you're going to
build a summary of all

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.750
of the issues in that project?

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.000
JEREMY LEWI: Actually, we
summarize individual issues.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.840
So one of the problems, if
you're maintaining a GitHub

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.930
project, is a lot of
times, people file issues

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.002
with uninformative
titles, in a large part

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:57.710
because maybe they're
filing a bug report

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.080
and they don't know what
the actual problem is.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.960
So after you do a
little bit of triaging

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.330
and you find out what
the actual problem is,

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.480
you want to go back and give the
issue a more informative title

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.940
to maintain the hygiene
of your project.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.262
But that requires a lot
of toil and manual labor.

00:04:13.262 --> 00:04:14.720
So this is something
that you might

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.680
want to automate by using ML
to see if you can automatically

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.519
generate a title from an issue.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:23.840
So as that issue gets updated,
as people provide comments

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.850
and feedback on the issue,
you can automatically

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:28.262
generate a title that better
summarizes that issue.

00:04:28.262 --> 00:04:30.470
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Will this
help with dedupe, perhaps?

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:34.640
Maybe duping different issues
in GitHub and helping developers

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.422
spend more time on the
issues that matter?

00:04:37.422 --> 00:04:39.380
JEREMY LEWI: It could be
extended, potentially,

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.210
to solve that problem.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.890
Because one of the things with
this particular application,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.422
you can actually learn
embeddings of GitHub issues,

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:51.380
and those can become
features for other models.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.088
And one of those models
could potentially

00:04:53.088 --> 00:04:55.642
be to identify duplicate issues.

00:04:55.642 --> 00:04:56.600
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.766
Is this something that we're
going to use, actually,

00:04:58.766 --> 00:05:01.070
at Google, perhaps, ourselves?

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:03.410
JEREMY LEWI: Right now it's
an exploratory product.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.020
So it's actually developed
by Hamal Hussein, who's

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.470
a data scientist at GitHub.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:11.120
And so we took
that and we started

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.880
to try to productionize
it using Kubeflow.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.130
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And what
are some of the technologies

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.360
that this is built on top of?

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.080
I assume, at least, Kubernetes,
maybe TensorFlow, right?

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.600
Anything else fit into
sort of the framework?

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.350
JEREMY LEWI: Yeah.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:25.933
So there's a lot of
different packages

00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:28.220
that we incorporate
to build this product.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.560
So we start off by
deploying JupyterHub,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.250
which is a multi-user
Jupyter notebook solution.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.160
And we use that to give our
data scientists the ability

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.870
to explore data and
build their models.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.270
And usually they're using
TensorFlow for that.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:46.280
When they're done doing
that, then they sort of

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.200
want to train at
scale, in this case,

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.900
using distributed TensorFlow
running on Kubernetes.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.010
They then have to build
and deploy a model.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.696
In this case, they're
using Selden I/O. And then

00:05:55.696 --> 00:05:57.320
after they deploy
that model, they also

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:01.080
have to deploy a simple
web application to actually

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.090
make it more consumable.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.590
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
So how hard is this

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:08.754
to set up for an end user
who has a GitHub repo?

00:06:08.754 --> 00:06:10.170
JEREMY LEWI: So
to set up Kubeflow

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.790
is just a couple of commands,
and we provide all the source

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:15.380
in our examples repo.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.690
So anybody should be able to
try to set it up and start

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.710
serving a model.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.440
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
five minutes to wow.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:21.622
JEREMY LEWI: Sure.

00:06:21.622 --> 00:06:22.580
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Great.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:23.630
Thanks, Jeremy.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.180
JEREMY LEWI: Thank you.

